A||N||5||Outkast||Yet Another Headlight Delimna||||5||
Z||000000||Outkast||01-11-2000||05:19 PM||||I have looked all over and scoured the message base, and all I have found is a mention of it but not a fix. Of course, I am blind in one eye and can't see out of the other. Anyway, my problem is this...when I turn on my headlights I have to spin the wheel on the motor to get it to start. Once I do that and The headlight raises everything is fine, until I turn them on the next time. The motor starts fine when I turn the lights off. Would this be a brush problem? And if so, are brushes still available? Thanks in advance. BTW, the car is an 86 SE.||||reg||5
Z||000001||MCordray||01-11-2000||06:36 PM||||I had the same problem a few months back. More than likely, it's an electrical problem. Mine started working normally after I disconnected and cleaned the relay terminals. Check to make sure you don't have a bad ground. Also, make sure your electrical connections are clean.||||reg||1
Z||000002||DRH||01-11-2000||07:02 PM||||My guesses would be a limit switch or a bad connection as mentioned.<P>For more info than anyone would ever want to know about Fiero headlights, check the site below...<P> <A HREF="http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm</A> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||86SE||01-12-2000||07:56 PM||||I have the same problem with my right headlight, I have to turn the nob on the motor and wind it up manually, but when I flip the switch for the lights to go down, they both go down fine........???????||||reg||9
Z||000004||Luperman||01-12-2000||11:50 PM||||have you tried swapping the relays from one headlight to the other to see if the problem goes to the other side?||||reg||10
Z||000005||Carrolles||01-13-2000||06:28 AM||||You didn't mention what year Fiero you have.  I have an 85SE.<P>On the older models (at least on mine) there is a set of limit switches attached to the commutator brush assembly inside the little rectangular cover on the side of the headlight actuator motor.  Most likely the contacts of the limit switch are either dirty or need to be bent to have a little more tension.<P>If you look at the link that DHR included below (http://www.tir.com/~jaski/headlight.htm)  it is listed as item number 7 and called Switch and Harness Assembly.<P>Pull this assembly out of the motor and check the reed switches.  These are limit switches that engage at the ends of the travel when the motor is going in either direction.  You might as well clean the brushes and the surface of the commutator while you are this far into the motor.  Also check to see that the connections where the wires attach to the switch assembly are tight.  One of mine had gotten a little loose where the wire entered the spade connector.  A dab of solder fixed that.<P>It's not a difficult job and does not require cracking open the motor case.  Just be sure and disconnect the negative battery cable before you start.  These motors have enough torque to do real damage to any body parts in the wrong place if the actuator starts up at the wrong time.<P>Good luck,<P>Carroll||||reg||1
